<main id="main">
<section class="page-hero"><img class="bg" src="/assets/img/photos/hero-home-after-rain.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/hero-home-after-rain-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/hero-home-after-rain.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.12"><div class="wrap"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span>Claim terms</nav><span class="kicker light fade-up">Reference</span><h1 class="fade-up" style="animation-delay:.06s">Insurance Claim Terms from A to Z</h1><p class="lede fade-up" style="animation-delay:.14s">45 words you’ll meet in policies, estimates and appraisal awards — including the flat-roof and adobe vocabulary specific to New Mexico.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><div class="filter"><label for="gloss-filter">Search the glossary</label><input id="gloss-filter" type="search" placeholder="Try “canale” or “umpire”"></div><div class="az"><a href="#g-A">A</a><a href="#g-C">C</a><a href="#g-D">D</a><a href="#g-E">E</a><a href="#g-H">H</a><a href="#g-L">L</a><a href="#g-M">M</a><a href="#g-N">N</a><a href="#g-O">O</a><a href="#g-P">P</a><a href="#g-R">R</a><a href="#g-S">S</a><a href="#g-U">U</a><a href="#g-W">W</a><a href="#g-X">X</a></div><dl class="glossary"><dt id="g-A">Actual cash value (ACV)</dt><dd>Replacement cost minus depreciation. Many policies pay ACV first and the balance after repairs are completed.</dd><dt>Additional living expense (ALE)</dt><dd>The extra cost of living elsewhere while a covered loss makes your home unlivable. It usually falls under Coverage D, loss of use.</dd><dt>Adobe</dt><dd>Sun-dried earthen block used in traditional New Mexico walls. Water and fire damage to adobe calls for specialized repair methods and pricing.</dd><dt>Amount of loss</dt><dd>The dollar value of the covered damage. It is the question an appraisal panel decides.</dd><dt>Appraisal award</dt><dd>The written decision signed by two of the three panel members that sets the amount of loss, ideally split into RCV, depreciation and ACV.</dd><dt>Appraisal demand</dt><dd>A written notice from you or the insurer invoking the policy’s appraisal clause because the parties disagree on the amount of loss.</dd><dt id="g-C">Canale</dt><dd>A projecting channel or spout that drains water off a flat roof through a parapet wall. Hail and wind can crack or loosen canales.</dd><dt>Competent and disinterested appraiser</dt><dd>The standard most appraisal clauses set: someone qualified to value the loss who has no financial stake in the outcome.</dd><dt>Coverage A (dwelling)</dt><dd>The part of a homeowners policy that covers the house itself and attached structures.</dd><dt>Coverage B (other structures)</dt><dd>Coverage for detached structures such as garages, coyote fences, sheds and walls.</dd><dt>Coverage C (personal property)</dt><dd>Coverage for your belongings, often called contents, subject to sublimits for certain categories.</dd><dt>Coverage D (loss of use)</dt><dd>Coverage for additional living expense or lost rental income while the property can’t be used after a covered loss.</dd><dt id="g-D">Debris removal</dt><dd>Coverage for hauling away damaged property and wreckage. After a wildfire it can be a major expense and may have its own limit.</dd><dt>Declarations page</dt><dd>The summary page of your policy showing limits, deductibles, the policy period and attached endorsements.</dd><dt>Depreciation</dt><dd>The reduction in value for age, wear and condition that separates replacement cost from actual cash value.</dd><dt id="g-E">Elastomeric roof coating</dt><dd>A flexible liquid-applied coating used on many flat roofs. Hail can fracture or puncture it, exposing the membrane below.</dd><dt>Endorsement</dt><dd>A form that adds to, removes or changes coverage in the base policy.</dd><dt>Evaporative cooler</dt><dd>A rooftop or ground-mounted swamp cooler common across New Mexico. Hail dents cabinets, and the roof jack and curb can leak.</dd><dt id="g-H">Holdback</dt><dd>The recoverable depreciation an insurer withholds until repairs or replacement are completed.</dd><dt id="g-L">Line item</dt><dd>A single priced entry in an estimate, such as a square of membrane or a linear foot of parapet cap.</dd><dt id="g-M">Matching</dt><dd>The question of whether undamaged materials must be replaced so repairs look uniform. Policy wording and New Mexico law both matter.</dd><dt>Microburst</dt><dd>A sudden, intense downdraft that produces damaging straight-line winds, often during monsoon storms.</dd><dt>Mitigation</dt><dd>Reasonable steps to prevent further damage after a loss, such as tarping a roof or drying out a wet room. Most policies require it.</dd><dt>Modified bitumen</dt><dd>An asphalt-based roll roofing membrane widely used on flat and low-slope roofs.</dd><dt>Monsoon</dt><dd>The summer storm season in New Mexico, roughly July through September, bringing hail, heavy rain, flash flooding and strong winds.</dd><dt id="g-N">New Mexico Property Insurance Program</dt><dd>The state’s FAIR plan, a market of last resort for property owners who can’t buy coverage in the regular market.</dd><dt id="g-O">Office of Superintendent of Insurance (OSI)</dt><dd>New Mexico’s insurance regulator. It licenses insurers and adjusters and takes consumer complaints.</dd><dt>Ordinance or law coverage</dt><dd>Coverage for added costs to bring repaired or rebuilt property up to current building codes, often subject to a percentage limit.</dd><dt>Overhead and profit (O&amp;P)</dt><dd>The general contractor’s overhead and profit, commonly 10 percent each, often disputed when several trades are involved.</dd><dt id="g-P">Parapet</dt><dd>The low wall extending above a flat roof, typical of Pueblo Revival and Territorial buildings. Its caps and flashing are frequent claim items.</dd><dt>Percentage deductible</dt><dd>A deductible figured as a percentage of the dwelling limit rather than a flat dollar amount, often applied to wind or hail.</dd><dt>Pitch</dt><dd>The slope of a roof, expressed as rise over 12 inches of run. Pitch affects area, labor and safety charges.</dd><dt>Proof of loss</dt><dd>A sworn statement of the amount claimed, which some policies require within a stated time after the insurer requests it.</dd><dt id="g-R">Recoverable depreciation</dt><dd>Depreciation the insurer will pay once you complete repairs or replacement, under a replacement cost policy.</dd><dt>Replacement cost value (RCV)</dt><dd>The cost to repair or replace damaged property with materials of like kind and quality, without deduction for depreciation.</dd><dt>Reservation of rights</dt><dd>A letter in which the insurer handles a claim while reserving the right to deny coverage later.</dd><dt id="g-S">Scope of loss</dt><dd>The list of damaged items and the repairs needed, before pricing. Many disputes are really scope disputes.</dd><dt>Smoke damage</dt><dd>Soot, odor and residue damage from fire. Wildfire smoke can affect a home that never burned, including HVAC systems and contents.</dd><dt>Square (roofing)</dt><dd>A unit of roof area equal to 100 square feet.</dd><dt>Stucco</dt><dd>A cement or synthetic exterior plaster finish common across New Mexico. Hail can fracture it, and matching texture and color is often disputed.</dd><dt>Sublimit</dt><dd>A lower limit inside a coverage for a specific category, such as jewelry, mold or debris removal.</dd><dt>Suit limitation</dt><dd>A policy provision that limits how long you have to sue the insurer. Check your policy and ask an attorney how New Mexico law treats it.</dd><dt id="g-U">Umpire</dt><dd>The neutral third member of an appraisal panel who decides items the two appraisers can’t agree on.</dd><dt id="g-W">Waste factor</dt><dd>An added percentage of material to cover cutting, overlap and fitting, especially on hip roofs and irregular layouts.</dd><dt id="g-X">Xactimate</dt><dd>Estimating software used by most insurers and many contractors to price repairs line by line with regional pricing.</dd></dl></article>
